**Edit:** If you're working on a real-world project and need to handle asynchronous actions (like fetching data from an API), Redux Toolkit provides a powerful utility called `createAsyncThunk`. Here’s a brief overview:

### Using `createAsyncThunk` for Asynchronous Actions

`createAsyncThunk` allows you to write asynchronous actions in a cleaner way.

#### Example: Fetching Data from an API

First, install the necessary dependencies:

```sh
npm install axios
```

Then, define an asynchronous action:

```javascript
// apiSlice.js
import { createAsyncThunk, createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';
import axios from 'axios';

export const fetchUsers = createAsyncThunk('users/fetchUsers', async () => {
  const response = await axios.get('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users');
  return response.data;
});

const usersSlice = createSlice({
  name: 'users',
  initialState: {
    loading: false,
    users: [],
    error: null,
  },
  extraReducers: (builder) => {
    builder
      .addCase(fetchUsers.pending, (state) => {
        state.loading = true;
        state.error = null;
      })
      .addCase(fetchUsers.fulfilled, (state, action) => {
        state.loading = false;
        state.users = action.payload;
      })
      .addCase(fetchUsers.rejected, (state, action) => {
        state.loading = false;
        state.error = action.error.message;
      });
  },
});

export default usersSlice.reducer;
```

#### Connecting to a React Component

Now, you can use this slice in a React component:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';
import { fetchUsers } from './apiSlice';

const UsersList = () => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const { loading, users, error } = useSelector((state) => state.users);

  const handleFetchUsers = () => {
    dispatch(fetchUsers());
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleFetchUsers}>Fetch Users</button>
      {loading && <p>Loading...</p>}
      {error && <p>Error: {error}</p>}
      {!loading && !error && (
        <ul>
          {users.map((user) => (
            <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default UsersList;
```

With `createAsyncThunk`, you can easily manage asynchronous operations and handle different states (pending, fulfilled, rejected) without writing boilerplate code.

