\section{Introduction}

The purpose of this paper is to study the structure of the group of homotopy classes of self-homotopy equivalences of a space $X$ that induce the identity on cohomology with coefficients in an abelian group $G$. We will denote this group by $\operatorname{Aut}_H^G(X)$. When $G=\mathbb Z$, we will write $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$ for short. 

There are two main motivations for studying such groups. First, it is natural to ask what kind of information one can extract from a space by looking at its homotopy type and its cohomology ring. In particular, how much can be recovered from just the cohomology ring? A well-known example of a result along these lines is the theorem of Whitehead \cite{W} stating that if $X$ and $Y$ are simply connected CW complexes with isomorphic cohomology rings, then they are homotopy equivalent provided the ranks of their homology groups are finite. Another example is the theorem of Kahn and Priddy \cite{KP}, which states that if $X$ is a suspension of a finite complex, then the order of $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$ divides the order of the stable Adams operations acting on the cohomology of $X$. These results are related to the problem of determining when two spaces have isomorphic cohomology rings (see \cite{CJ} for a survey of the subject). The second motivation comes from the theory of formal spaces. Let us recall that a simply connected CW complex $X$ is called formal if its rationalization $X_{(0)}$ has the same minimal model as a product of Eilenberg-Mac Lane spaces. Equivalently, $X$ is formal if there is a quasi-isomorphism between the Sullivan model of $X$ and the tensor algebra of the rational cohomology of $X$ (see \cite{FHT}). It is known that a formal space is rigid in the sense that any homotopy class of self-maps inducing the identity on cohomology must be homotopic to the identity (see \cite{K} or \cite[Proposition 13.4]{FHT}). In other words, $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)=\{1\}$ if $X$ is formal. This raises the question of whether the converse is true. For instance, does $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)=\{1\}$ imply formality for $X$? The answer is no, but the question is interesting enough that it has been studied in several papers. See for instance \cite{B}, \cite{BCS}, \cite{D}, \cite{DS}, \cite{M}, \cite{O}, \cite{R}. 

The above discussion shows that the group $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$ contains some information about the space $X$ that may not be obvious from its cohomology ring alone. However, this information is often difficult to compute. For example, it is easy to see that if $X$ is a wedge of spheres, then $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$ is trivial. But it is a non-trivial fact that if $X$ is a wedge of Moore spaces $M(\mathbb Z/p,n)$ with $n>2$, then $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$ is also trivial (see \cite{B}). The same holds for wedges of odd-dimensional spheres and Moore spaces $M(\mathbb Z/p,2m)$ with $p$ odd and $m>1$ (see \cite{O}). There are also some results on the size of $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$ for certain families of spaces, including lens spaces (see \cite{B}), homogeneous spaces (see \cite{BCS}), and finite CW complexes (see \cite{M}). 

In this paper we will focus on the case where $X$ is a sphere or a Moore space. The reason is that these spaces are simple enough that one can hope to say something more precise than just giving a bound on the size of $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$. The case of spheres was studied in \cite{B}, where it was shown that if $X$ is an odd-dimensional sphere, then $\operatorname{Aut}_H(X)$ is either trivial or cyclic of order 2. Moreover, the latter occurs only for $X=S^{2m+1}$ with $m\geq 1$. For even-dimensional spheres, the situation is less clear. The group $\operatorname{Aut}_H(S^{2n})$ is known to be trivial for $n=1$ and $n=2$ (see \cite{B}), but the general case remains open. Our first result is the following.

\begin{thm}\label{main}
Let $X$ be a Moore space $M(A,n)$ with $n>2$. Then $\operatorname{Aut}_H^A(X)$ is trivial.
\end{thm}

This extends the result mentioned above for Moore spaces $M(\mathbb Z/p,2m)$ with $p$ odd and $m>1$. In Section 2 we prove Theorem \ref{main} and use it to show that $\operatorname{Aut}_H(S^{2n})$ is trivial for $n\leq 3$. In Section 3 we study the case where $X$ is a sphere or a Moore space and $G$ is a finite abelian group. Our main result is the following.

\begin{thm}\label{main2}
Let $X$ be a sphere or a Moore space and let $G$ be a finite abelian group. Then $\operatorname{Aut}_H^G(X)$ is finite.
\end{thm}

We do not know if the finiteness statement in Theorem \ref{main2} holds for all spaces $X$. In Section 4 we give some examples of spaces $X$ for which $\operatorname{Aut}_H^G(X)$ is infinite for some $G$. Finally, in Section 5 we discuss some possible extensions of our results.

