Sure! The `@Value` annotation in Spring is used to inject values into fields, setter methods, or constructors of beans. These values can be specified in the Spring configuration file or as default values.

Here's an example of using `@Value` to inject a value from a property file:

```java
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public MyService myService() {
        return new MyServiceImpl();
    }
}

@Component
public class MyServiceImpl implements MyService {
    private String myProperty;

    @Value("${my.property}")
    public void setMyProperty(String myProperty) {
        this.myProperty = myProperty;
    }

    // Use the injected value...
}
```

In this example, we have a `MyServiceImpl` class that has a `String` field called `myProperty`. We use the `@Value` annotation to inject the value of `my.property` from a properties file into the `setMyProperty` method.

Note that we need to specify the property name in the curly braces (`{}`) after `@Value`.

Also, if the property is not found in the properties file, the `@Value` annotation can also provide a default value like this: 

```java
@Value("${my.property:default_value}")
```

This will inject the value of `my.property` if it exists, otherwise it will inject `default_value`.

