I have a simple windows application that I want to display the username of the current logged on user. How do I do this? You can use the `Environment.UserName` property to get the name of the current user:

```csharp
string userName = Environment.UserName;
Console.WriteLine("Current User: " + userName);
```

This will give you the username of the currently logged-in Windows user.

If you need more detailed information about the user, such as domain and full profile details, you might want to use the `System.Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity` class:

```csharp
using System.Security.Principal;

WindowsIdentity identity = WindowsIdentity.GetCurrent();
WindowsPrincipal principal = new WindowsPrincipal(identity);
string userName = identity.Name;
Console.WriteLine("Current User: " + userName);
```

This approach provides more comprehensive information and is useful if you need additional context about the user's security settings or roles. However, for most basic needs, `Environment.UserName` should suffice. 

Make sure to include the necessary namespaces at the top of your file:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Principal;
```

Here's a complete example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Principal;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Using Environment.UserName
        string userName1 = Environment.UserName;
        Console.WriteLine("Using Environment.UserName: " + userName1);

        // Using WindowsIdentity
        WindowsIdentity identity = WindowsIdentity.GetCurrent();
        string userName2 = identity.Name;
        Console.WriteLine("Using WindowsIdentity: " + userName2);
    }
}
```

Both methods will provide the username of the current user running the application. Choose the one that best fits your needs. If you just need the username, `Environment.UserName` is simpler and sufficient. If you need more detailed information, consider using `WindowsIdentity`. 

