\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The detection of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) polarization at degree angular scales has been a major milestone in observational cosmology. The first detection was reported by the \textit{Wilkinson Microwave Anisotropy Probe} (WMAP) \citep{bennett2013nine}. Since then, the \textit{Planck} satellite \citep{planck2016lil} and the ground-based experiments BICEP2/Keck Array \citep{aghanim2018planck, bicep2015bicep} and SPTpol \citep{sptpol2017spt} have provided high-resolution measurements of the CMB polarization.

The main goal of these observations is to detect the primordial B-mode signal generated during inflation \citep{seljak1997cosmological, kaiser1994gravitational}. The B-modes are expected to be generated by gravitational waves from inflation, which induce a curl component in the CMB polarization field. The amplitude of this signal is proportional to the energy scale of inflation, and therefore its detection would provide an unprecedented insight into the physics of the early universe. However, the measurement of the B-mode signal is challenging because it is extremely weak compared to other sources of polarization such as the E-mode signal and the polarized foreground emission from our Galaxy. Moreover, instrumental systematics can also mimic a primordial B-mode signal \citep[e.g.][]{kogut2007systematic, smith2012systematics, abdalla2014systematic}.

In order to reduce the impact of systematic effects, most of the current CMB polarization experiments adopt a two-frequency approach, where each detector measures the Stokes $Q$ and $U$ parameters of the CMB polarization at two different frequencies. The idea is that the contamination from Galactic foregrounds can be mitigated by combining the data from different frequencies, since the spectral behavior of the Galactic emission is known. On the other hand, the instrumental systematics are assumed to be frequency-independent, and therefore they can be removed by combining the data from different frequencies. However, if the systematics are not perfectly stable across frequencies, they will leak into the B-mode signal and cause a spurious detection. This is particularly problematic for the B-mode signal, which is expected to be very weak, and any leakage from systematics could be mistaken for a primordial B-mode signal. In fact, the first claimed detection of the primordial B-mode signal by the BICEP2/Keck Array collaboration \citep{bicep2014bicep} was later shown to be contaminated by Galactic dust emission \citep{aghanim2018planck, bicep2015bicep}.

The sensitivity of the current CMB polarization experiments is limited by the noise level of the detectors. The next generation of CMB experiments aim to improve the sensitivity by using more detectors and by deploying them in more telescopes. For example, the Simons Observatory (SO) project plans to use 60 telescopes \citep{simonsobservatory2019}, while the CMB-S4 experiment will deploy about 100 telescopes \citep{abazajian2016c}. The large number of telescopes makes it possible to combine data from multiple telescopes, which can help to mitigate the impact of systematics. However, the combination of data from multiple telescopes also introduces additional challenges, such as the need to account for the different pointing and scanning strategies of the telescopes.

In this paper, we present a new method to combine data from multiple telescopes while mitigating the impact of systematics. Our method is based on the use of a set of templates that represent the expected systematics in the data. These templates are used to model the systematics in the data and to remove them before combining the data from different telescopes. We show that our method can significantly reduce the impact of systematics and improve the sensitivity of the combined data. We apply our method to simulated data from the SO experiment and show that it can achieve a factor of $\sim$2 improvement in the sensitivity to the B-mode signal compared to the standard combination method.

This paper is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:method}, we describe our method to combine data from multiple telescopes while mitigating the impact of systematics. In Section~\ref{sec:simulation}, we describe the simulated data used in our analysis. In Section~\ref{sec:result}, we present the results of our analysis and compare them with the standard combination method. Finally, in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion}, we discuss the implications of our results and the future work.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

Our method is based on the use of a set of templates that represent the expected systematics in the data. These templates are used to model the systematics in the data and to remove them before combining the data from different telescopes. The basic idea is to use the templates to estimate the contribution of the systematics to the data and to subtract it out, leaving only the signal of interest. We assume that the systematics are frequency-independent, so we can use the same templates to model the systematics at all frequencies. We also assume that the templates are known up to a multiplicative factor, which can be estimated from the data.

Let us denote the observed Stokes $Q$ and $U$ parameters at frequency $\nu_i$ as $Q_i(\theta)$ and $U_i(\theta)$, where $\theta$ represents the sky position. The true Stokes parameters $Q_{\rm true}(\theta)$ and $U_{\rm true}(\theta)$ are related to the observed parameters through the following equation:
\begin{equation}
\begin{pmatrix}
Q_i(\theta) \\
U_i(\theta)
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
Q_{\rm true}(\theta) \\
U_{\rm true}(\theta)
\end{pmatrix}
+
\sum_{j=1}^N c_j
\begin{pmatrix}
T_j(\theta) \\
T_j(\theta)
\end{pmatrix},
\label{eq:data_model}
\end{equation}
where $T_j(\theta)$ are the templates representing the systematics, and $c_j$ are the coefficients that need to be estimated from the data. We assume that the templates are known up to a multiplicative factor, so we can write $T_j(\theta) = f_j t_j(\theta)$, where $t_j(\theta)$ are the normalized templates and $f_j$ are the unknown factors. The goal is to estimate the true Stokes parameters $Q_{\rm true}(\theta)$ and $U_{\rm true}(\theta)$ by removing the contribution of the systematics.

To estimate the coefficients $c_j$, we can use a least-squares fit to the data. Let us define the data vector $\mathbf{d}$ as the concatenation of the observed Stokes parameters at all frequencies:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{d} =
\begin{pmatrix}
Q_1(\theta) \\
U_1(\theta) \\
\vdots \\
Q_N(\theta) \\
U_N(\theta)
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Similarly, let us define the template matrix $\mathbf{T}$ as the concatenation of the templates at all frequencies:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{T} =
\begin{pmatrix}
t_1(\theta) & \cdots & t_N(\theta) \\
t_1(\theta) & \cdots & t_N(\theta) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
t_1(\theta) & \cdots & t_N(\theta) \\
t_1(\theta) & \cdots & t_N(\theta)
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Then, the data model can be written as:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{Q}_{\rm true} + \mathbf{T} \mathbf{c},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{Q}_{\rm true}$ is the true Stokes parameter vector and $\mathbf{c}$ is the coefficient vector. To estimate the coefficients $\mathbf{c}$, we can use the following least-squares solution:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{c} = (\mathbf{T}^\top \mathbf{T})^{-1} \mathbf{T}^\top (\mathbf{d} - \mathbf{Q}_{\rm true}).
\end{equation}
Once we have estimated the coefficients $\mathbf{c}$, we can remove the contribution of the systematics from the data by subtracting $\mathbf{T} \mathbf{c}$ from $\mathbf{d}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{d}_{\rm sys} = \mathbf{d} - \mathbf{T} \mathbf{c}.
\end{equation}
The resulting data vector $\mathbf{d}_{\rm sys}$ contains only the true Stokes parameters and the noise. We can then combine the data from different telescopes by averaging the corresponding elements of $\mathbf{d}_{\rm sys}$.

