The game is now almost complete. The only thing that's missing is the ability to pause the game. Pause the game by clicking the Pause button.

To pause and resume the game, we'll use the NSTimer object that controls the movement of the ball. When the user clicks the Pause button, we'll invalidate the timer, stopping the game. When the user clicks Resume, we'll recreate the timer, starting the game again.

First, add an action method to the interface for handling clicks on the Pause button:

@interface BreakoutGame : NSWindowController {
    NSTimer *gameTimer;

- (IBAction)pauseGame:(id)sender;
- (IBAction)resumeGame:(id)sender;

Next, implement the two new methods:

- (IBAction)pauseGame:(id)sender
    [gameTimer invalidate];
    gameTimer = nil;

- (IBAction)resumeGame:(id)sender
    gameTimer = [NSTimer scheduledTimerWithTimeInterval:0.02
                                                  target:self selector:@selector(updateGame:)
                                                userInfo:nil repeats:YES];

Notice that the resumeGame: method uses the same selector as the gameTimer object in the updateGame: method. That's because both methods are responsible for moving the ball and checking for collisions.

To complete the game, connect the Pause button to the pauseGame: method, and connect the Resume button to the resumeGame: method. Then run your application and try it out!

Where To Go From Here?

Congratulations! You've just built a fully functional breakout game in Cocoa. But you're not limited to just this simple version; there are many ways you can expand upon the game. For example, you could add more levels to the game, each with different brick layouts. Or, you could give the player a finite number of lives instead of just one. You could even allow the user to create his own levels. The possibilities are endless!

